Both of these are while-helpless messages. Dragging an iron ball leaves
you helpless for the rest of the turn, plus one more turn (so if you're
at normal speed, dragging an iron ball on turn 4 means you next move at
turn 6); this is also the reason you can't run while punished, unless
you take the ball with you. Many people don't pay close attention to the
T: counter and so don't realise that the iron ball is costing them so
many turns. While moving (rather than while acting in place, as might
happen during combat), it's better to be Burdened carrying the ball,
than unburdened with the ball on the ground. The "while dragging an iron
ball" appears if you die during the helplessness period after moving it.

As for jumping, doing that leaves you helpless for the rest of the
current turn. Unless you're regularly going around as an air elemental
or the like, this is almost impossible to notice; you don't get a bonus
action every turn even when "very fast", so you'd have to be paying a
lot of attention to notice that you were getting fewer bonus actions
than expected. The "while jumping around" message appears (on NAO or
other variants with the while-helpless patch included) if you die after
jumping, but before the current turn ends (for any reason, most commonly
a monster acting after you and killing you through HP damage).

